.TH MINIAS 1 "May 2026" "minias"
.SH NAME
minias \- small x86-64 assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minias
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR iter ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ input ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minias
assembles AT&T-syntax x86-64 assembly into an ELF64 relocatable object.
It is intended to support a reasonable practical set of assembly,
including compiler output from GCC, Clang, cproc/QBE, and chibicc,
rather than to
implement every feature of GNU
.BR as (1).
.PP
If
.I input
is omitted or is
.BR \- ,
.B minias
reads assembly from standard input.  If
.B \-o
is omitted, the object file is written to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the generated object file to
.IR output .
.TP
.BI \-r " iter"
Run
.I iter
jump relaxation passes after the initial assembly pass.
The default is 1.
.SH INPUT SYNTAX
.B minias
accepts a practical subset of GNU-style AT&T x86-64 assembly, including
labels, local numeric labels, common ELF directives, relocations, data
directives, and instruction forms used by real programs.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B minias
only targets x86-64 ELF relocatable output.
It does not aim to assemble other architectures or every extension
accepted by GNU
.BR as (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
minias -o file.o file.s
cc -S -O2 file.c -o - | minias -o file.o -
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR as (1),
.BR cc (1)
